---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../data/profile";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Pirate Arcade — a side project and workbench experiment in zero-cost AI-assisted game development."
  current="about"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__header">
      <p class="section__eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="section__title">The Project</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Pirate Arcade is a collection of four classic arcade games reimagined
        with a pirate nautical theme. It's also a workbench experiment: how
        far can a zero-cost AI-assisted development workflow go when you
        combine free coding tools, local hardware, and open source
        infrastructure?
      </p>

      <p>
        The games themselves are desktop apps built with Python and Pygame —
        fully playable, cross-platform, and released under the MIT license.
        This website is the public face of the project, serving as both a
        landing page and a build journal.
      </p>

      <h2>The Stack</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>AI assistance:</strong> OpenCode with free-to-use models
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Desktop games:</strong> Python, Pygame, NumPy (procedural
          audio)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Website:</strong> Astro, TypeScript, vanilla CSS, Cloudflare
          Pages
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Infrastructure:</strong> GitHub (source, CI, releases),
          Cloudflare free tier (hosting)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hardware:</strong> Local X600 workstation, no cloud compute
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why Pirate Arcade?</h2>
      <p>
        Because building things is more fun when you add pirates. Also:
        classic arcade games are well-understood problems, which makes them
        ideal benchmarks for evaluating AI-assisted coding tools. Every game
        here is a data point.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Isn't</h2>
      <p>
        This is not a commercial game studio. Not a startup. Not a
        subscription product. It's a side project — a workspace for exploring
        what's possible when you remove budget constraints from the
        development process and focus on what a single person with good tools
        can ship.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__header">
      <p class="section__eyebrow">Maker</p>
      <h2 class="section__title">Made by Erich Donahue</h2>
      <p class="section__description">
        Decision science, data products, and practical experiments.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I build practical decision tools and small creative systems. This
        project is my workbench for exploring AI-assisted development
        workflows — what works, what doesn't, and how the process changes
        when code generation is essentially free.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href={site.authorUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          erichdonahue.com
        </a>
        {" \u2014 "}my personal site, built with the same stack and a record-store
        visual language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
